00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.690 align:start position:0%
 
I'm very keen to talk to you about rail  strikes of course but I just want to  start with a story that the times is  reporting which is saying that the  limits on taking liquids into airports  onto airplanes are going to be stopped  by 2024 is that correct well I  understand that stories off the back of  some commentary from one of the bosses  of an airport and I'm afraid you'll know  our usual practice on security matters  is we don't comment on security matters  I think it's quite important I'm now  responsible for setting the security  regime for our Aviation sector so you  know if there are any changes coming  we'll set those out for people in due  course  um but at the moment the the regime is  as it is with the strict limits on the  limit liquids people can take on planes  I mean the times are saying that because  of advancements in Scanners the limits  are going to be unnecessary I mean that  would be quite welcome to use for  travelers wouldn't it well look I'm sure  I'm sure people would like to not have  those limits but they're in place for a  very good reason if that should change  in the future then obviously we'll set  that out but you know the security  regime is in place for a reason it's  important that people follow it  um you know we always keep these matters  under review but at the moment the  position is as it is okay and now you're  meeting with the rmt tomorrow the rail  Union what's your message going to be  well look the reason for meeting very  clearly I'm not negotiating with them  that's very clearly for the trade unions  and the employers Network Rail and the  train operating companies but I do think  in this in this case it's important to  meet with the unions um this isn't just  these these strikes are not just about  pay this is about long-running talks  that are actually about delivering rail  reform people will know that because of  the pandemic significant number of  people have stopped using Railways you  know the number of passengers is around  80 percent of what it was pre-pandemic  that set up some very significant  financial challenges for the industry I  want a thriving viable financially  sustainable Railway that's the only way  you actually secure the jobs of the  rmt's members so it's the same same  shared agenda and I want to set that out  to Mr Lynch and the other Trade union  leaders about that being our ambition  and I would urge him and the other trade  unions to get around the table I'd urge  them to call off the strikes get back  around the table with the employers try  and Hammer out some of those reforms  that are necessary in which deliver the  savings that can then help pay for pay  Rises for his members and deliver a  better service you know these strikes  are going to be damaging for passengers  they're going to be redisruptive for the  first Christmas we've had that hasn't  been impacted by covid a big hit for the  hospitality sector how much will it cost  the economy you think well look it it's  going to be a very significant cost it's  going to be no I haven't but we've  already heard some very significant cost  estimates from the hospital from UK  Hospitality you know that's a sector  that was hit during covid you know I  spoke up for that sector when I was on  the back benches you know the Prime  Minister when he was Chancellor tried to  protect that sector and these rail  strikes are going to be very damaging  can be damaging for the people in the  rail industry who are going to lose  their pay and their their overtime but  they're going to also hit people in the  hospitality sector they're going to be  damaging for passengers and I would  still urge the unions to call off the  strikes get back around the table and  try to hammer out an agreement and  that's what I'm going to be saying to Mr  Lynch when I meet him tomorrow I mean  you know you're saying there that you're  not involved in the talks that they're  between the rmt and the train operating  companies I guess some of our viewers  will be thinking hang on you're the  transport secretary why don't you get  involved surely this is exactly the  minute for you to actually try and make  a difference you know just look at the  dates of the strikes the 13th 14th 16th  17th of December the third fourth sixth  seventh of January just when people as  you say hospitality industry people can  be trying to find their fam going to see  their families they're going to be  wanting to get into the Christmas spirit  why don't you get involved well look  they are very damaging and and I am  involved in the sense I'm going to set  out very clearly what our ambition is  why it's important that the unions reach  a deal with the employers but the detail  matters here so it's about the specific  reforms that Network rail need to see in  their maintenance operations it's about  the specific reformers we need to see in  having a seven-day Railway more flexible  Railway and it's about pay as well what  do you think of inflation inflation's 11  is it fair for uh transport workers to  be expecting to see their pay matching  inflation well look the difficult  challenge that we've seen big challenge  for the economy uh because of covid so  that was a you know one in 300 year hit  to the economy and we're recovering from  that we've seen a uh at the made in  Russia challenge you know because of  their illegal invasions do you think  that the pay for transport workers  should match so so the reason why I'm  putting in context is because I think  the context matters  um the most important thing for  everybody in the country whether you're  a transport worker or or some other  public sector worker or someone in the  private set is that we get inflation  under control that's why the chancellor  to set out a credible economic package  last week  so that's the concept well and so that  means that although I completely  understand why people facing these cost  of living pressures want more pay the  government is simply not in a position  to pay inflation pay Rises and if we  were to do so the danger is that we  would embed inflation and that wouldn't  actually be in anyone's long-term  interest what's in people's interests is  that we get inflation driven out of the  system so that it comes back down to a  lower level we see interest rates then  falling that's how we get a long-term  sustainable position I accept it's  difficult for people and a Chancellor  made that clear last week none of this  is easy but there is a context here and  I think that's important for for the  rail unions to understand as well as  other people who Who oversee are  pressing for higher pay Rises now MIT  Lynch of course takes a different  position on this and many other things  as well to be fair now I just want to  have a look at what he said earlier he  said the Secretary of State the rather  than City on his hands has got to get  down to the serious business of creating  a settlement yesterday I was told by the  most senior person working for the  training operation companies that he's  been stopped from offered offering us a  deal he's being prevented from doing  that by the Department of Transport and  it seems they're stuck on the idea of  continuing this dispute into the new  year is that right that the DFT in the  department for transport and yourself as  a secretary of state are blocking the  train operating companies for offering  no look at the end of that quote there  we're Mick Lynch was talking about us  wanting this to go into new we  absolutely do not I I don't want any  strikes to take place  I want the dispute to be settled so I'm  not going to get into  um you know throwing accusations around  I want to have a sensible conversation  with him tomorrow where I set out the  context of what we're trying to achieve  which is recognizing the railway  Industries had a big hit because of the  pound the imperative is to get reform so  that we get more people using the  railway we drive up Revenue we drive out  cost we deliver the ability for the  train operating companies and network  rail to be able to offer better salaries  and wages to the people that work in the  industry and that can only be achieved  by the trade unions in the employers  getting around the table and hammering  out those reforms and I want to I want  to try and help facilitate that and  that's the message that I'm going to be  delivering to him tomorrow now in other  news the Supreme Court ruled today that  the Scottish government can't call an  independence referendum on its own  unilaterally Westminster has to agree to  it as well  so if the democratically elected  Scottish government requests permission  to call an independence referendum would  you grant it well look the government  made it very clear today the Prime  Minister has asked this question it will  vary in solvent prime minister's  discretion State and he made it very  clear look the priority for I think the  people of Scotland and indeed everyone  in the UK is to focus on the issues  facing the country which is deal with  the economic challenge protect people  from energy price Rises  um and the consequences of the war in  Ukraine and that requires the UK  government and the Scottish government  working together and I think the really  um a positive thing that the Prime  Minister did he was the first Prime  Minister for many years to go to the  British Irish Council he sat down with  um the Scottish first minister they had  a very sensible conversation about about  priorities and I think that partnership  working is what we need to see and  that's that's the priority for the UK  government you talk about the priorities  of the Scottish people but Scotland has  voted for the Scottish national party  that they argue is their mandate I mean  the clues in the name of the Scottish  national party they're not standing on a  platform to do anything other than  deliver Independence well I think if you  look at actually what the Scottish  people want they want the government  actually to be focused on both  governments to be focused on the  day-to-day challenges they voted in the  Scottish national party who is in their  manifestation to use their powers and  you know the Scottish Parliament has a  tremendous set of powers to deal with  the things that it is responsible for  which is you know running the Health  Service in Scotland running the  education system in Scotland delivering  better Services is there a line which  you would say okay this is when a  referendum should be allowed what is  that red line is it for example you know  the s p getting a majority in the  Scottish Parliament well look our  position is very clear we think the  position at the moment is that the UK  government the Scottish government  should be working together focused on  the priorities that are facing the  United Kingdom is their route to a  referendum that you can spell out well  look we we had a a referendum in 2014  and the the position that was agreed and  that was settled not not just between uh  Westminster and the Scottish government  but actually agreed across Civic  Scotland was that that was a once in a  generation referendum and we've  respected the result of that referendum  and that referendum was that Scotland  stayed in the United Kingdom and that  means Scotland has two governments it  has a Scottish government and it has the  UK government and those governments  should work together I understand the  results of the referendum but the  question is is there a route to another  referendum well the priority at the  moment is for the two governments to  work together on the issues facing the  United Kingdom  and those are the issues that I set out  which are the economic challenges  protecting people from energy price  Rises and dealing with the economic  challenges and then delivering on the  public services that we deliver you know  the UK government's responsible for  delivering those in England the Scottish  government's responsible for delivering  those in Scotland and the Prime Minister  will work with the first minister in  Partnership to deliver on those  priorities for for the people in both  parts of the United Kingdom okay thank  you very much indeed for being on the  program pleasure

